A pairwise check can discard one person at every step. When `candidate` knows `person`, the candidate cannot qualify. When the candidate does not know that person, the other person cannot qualify because a celebrity must be known by everyone.

After this scan, the code checks the survivor against every row and column. This also handles a matrix where no celebrity exists.

```go
func celebrity(M [][]int) int {
	n := len(M)
	if n == 0 {
		return -1
	}

	candidate := 0
	for person := 1; person < n; person++ {
		if M[candidate][person] == 1 {
			candidate = person
		}
	}

	for person := 0; person < n; person++ {
		if person == candidate {
			continue
		}
		if M[candidate][person] == 1 || M[person][candidate] == 0 {
			return -1
		}
	}

	return candidate
}
```

For a dry run, start with candidate 0. If person 0 does not know person 1, person 1 is removed. If person 0 knows person 2, person 0 is removed and candidate 2 continues. Only the final check decides whether that survivor is real.

An input with no people returns -1, and an input with several valid-looking candidates still returns -1 unless one person satisfies both rules.

Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(1)

This version puts all indexes into a stack and compares two people at a time. If `a` knows `b`, then `a` cannot be the answer. If `a` does not know `b`, then `b` cannot be the answer. One possible person remains, and the second loop verifies that person.

It uses more memory than the one-candidate scan, but the elimination rule is easy to follow.

```python
class Solution:
    def celebrity(self, M):
        n = len(M)
        if n == 0:
            return -1

        people = list(range(n))

        while len(people) > 1:
            first = people.pop()
            second = people.pop()

            if M[first][second] == 1:
                people.append(second)
            else:
                people.append(first)

        candidate = people[0]

        for person in range(n):
            if person == candidate:
                continue
            if M[candidate][person] == 1 or M[person][candidate] == 0:
                return -1

        return candidate
```

An edge case is a single-person matrix. That person passes the verification because there are no other people to check.

Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(n)

The first pass removes people who cannot be celebrities. If the current candidate knows another person, the candidate is invalid. Otherwise, that other person is invalid because the candidate does not know them.

The remaining index still needs a full check. Finding a candidate alone is not enough.

```java
class Solution {
    public int celebrity(int[][] M) {
        int n = M.length;
        if (n == 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        int candidate = 0;
        for (int person = 1; person < n; person++) {
            if (M[candidate][person] == 1) {
                candidate = person;
            }
        }

        for (int person = 0; person < n; person++) {
            if (person == candidate) {
                continue;
            }
            if (M[candidate][person] == 1 || M[person][candidate] == 0) {
                return -1;
            }
        }

        return candidate;
    }
}
```

Dry run for `[ [0,1,0], [0,0,0], [1,1,0] ]`: candidate 0 knows person 1, so candidate becomes 1. Person 1 knows nobody, and both other people know person 1, so the answer is 1.

Time complexity: O(n)
Space complexity: O(1)

This keeps the direct method. For every person, the code checks the whole row and column. A celebrity must know nobody else, and everybody else must know that person.

```cpp
class Solution
{
public:
    int celebrity(vector<vector<int>> &M){
        int n = M.size();

        for (int person = 0; person < n; person++) {
            bool knowsNobody = true;
            bool knownByEverybody = true;

            for (int other = 0; other < n; other++) {
                if (person != other && M[person][other] == 1) {
                    knowsNobody = false;
                }
                if (person != other && M[other][person] == 0) {
                    knownByEverybody = false;
                }
            }

            if (knowsNobody && knownByEverybody) {
                return person;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

For example, if person 2 has a row of zeroes except the diagonal, and every other row has a 1 in column 2, person 2 is returned. An empty matrix also returns -1.

Time complexity: O(n^2)
Space complexity: O(1)

Here the matrix is treated as a directed graph. A 1 from row `i` to column `j` means person `i` knows person `j`. A celebrity has zero outgoing links and incoming links from all other people, so the code counts both values for each row and column.

This is not the constant-space version, but it makes the two required conditions very visible.

```javascript
class Solution {
    celebrity(M) {
        const n = M.length;
        if (n === 0) {
            return -1;
        }

        const outgoing = new Array(n).fill(0);
        const incoming = new Array(n).fill(0);

        for (let person = 0; person < n; person++) {
            for (let other = 0; other < n; other++) {
                if (person !== other && M[person][other] === 1) {
                    outgoing[person]++;
                    incoming[other]++;
                }
            }
        }

        for (let person = 0; person < n; person++) {
            if (outgoing[person] === 0 && incoming[person] === n - 1) {
                return person;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

Dry run with three people: if the incoming counts are `[1, 2, 0]` and the outgoing counts are `[1, 0, 2]`, person 1 has zero outgoing links and two incoming links, so person 1 is the celebrity.

Time complexity: O(n^2)
Space complexity: O(n)

